Date: Fri, 11 Jun 93 13:22:16 BST
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: QM: Theories vs. Interpretations

Timothy May writes:
>Saying that someday, somehow, someone will devise a test is certainly
>possible, but this does not a falsifiable theory make. If, for example, I
>assert that ghostly spirits control the motions of subatomic particles, and
>that I believe that someday a "ghost detector" will allow us to measure
>whether ghosts are present or not, this surely would not make my theory a
>"testable" one!

In the scenario I imagine you are imagining, I suspect that the terms
"ghostly spirits" and "ghost detector" would not have been given any
meaning.  Unless the theory includes some account of the nature of its
"ghostly spirits" beyond merely being that which moves the particles
around, one might substitute "fnurly spirits" and "fnurl detector"
throughout the theory and have an equivalent theory.

In contrast, a theory that predicts, for example, that certain undiscovered
particles should exist, but only at energies which we cannot currently even
guess at a technological path to attaining, seems reasonably described as
"testable" in the context of this discussion.

Replace "ghostly spirits" by "hidden variables" and you get an untestable
theory.  Replace "ghostly spirits" by "hidden variables satisfying
such-and-such equations", and you may or may not have a theory
experimentally distinguishable in principle from standard QM, depending on
the particular equations.

