I agree completely with Nick! What Drexler has done is very important,
in terms of examining the possible limits to miniaturization and the
advantages of molecular machines. 

But all the hand-wringing I often see about why companies like Intel
and Apple don't have large nanotech efforts is misguided. At this
time, the best "nanotech" work is going on in polymer chemistry,
protein engineering, and other branches of biotech, plus a tiny bit in
AFMs and ATMs. Pretty far from the gears and bearings of
"Nanosystems," and not really "nanotechnology" at all, but it's where
the money is.

Intel, as I so often write about (got to use experience!), is not
going to replace the 486 or 586 with a rod-logic computer anytime
soon, nor are "biocomputers" on the horizon. As Nick points out, there
is no identifiable path to the world of full-blown nanotechnology.

Being a "Technology Platonist," I believe the machines Drexler
describes are "out there" in our future, someplace. When the
technology precursers have been built, when the economic foundations
have been laid, the developments will occur...the space will be
explored, the richer veins of ore will be mined, the frontiers will be
pushed out.

--Tim May


