Robin Hanson says:
> >As for military force, I suspect that SDI like systems work a whole
> >lot better when its a three day trip to reach you on the moon.
> 
> How so?  A warhead en route to your asteriod colony would be *very*
> hard to see until it almost got there.  No air to make it glow, and
> small course corrections could translate into large displacements over
> large distances.

Phased Array Radar covers a myriad of ills. In particular, given not
altogether that much money, you can easily track virtually everything
in a pretty big chunk of space with one phased array unit -- with
several deployed in orbit, I expect that you could track everything
within several light seconds. Course corrections could of course do
what you say -- make it look like you are going to point A when you
are going to point B, but they don't cause 180 degree shifts in course
-- they cause fairly small ones.  The computer running your phased
array can easily figure out what is and isn't a possible threat.

This is not to say that countermeasures like lasing your phased array
units aren't possible, but there ARE things you can do.

Perry

