I've stayed out of the vegan debate, except for my one Klaus! comment
about not being from Vega, but I share the interests here in vitamins
and antioxidants.

A good book, I've found, is Dr. Michael Colgan's "Optimum Sports
Nutrition," Advance Research Press, 1993. Available in nutrition and
health food stores.

Here's what I take, for what it's worth:

- a scoop of "All-1" in juice (15 g = 30 cal, 7 g of protein, 1 g of
carbo, 0 g of fat, 10,000 IU of A, 6000 IU of Beta Carotene, 1 g of C,
21 amino acids, lots of minerals, plus a bunch of other stuff....read
the label at a sports/health/nutrition store)

- before workouts at health club,  a scoop of Highland's "Intense
Workout" in juice (30 g = 113 cal, 7 g of protein, 20 g of carbo, 1 g
of mediuym chain triglycerides, 50 mg of inosine, phosphates, fatigue
fighters, lots of minerals and vitamins, boron, etc.

- I also pop a few more grams of C during the day, a couple of 400 IU
E capsules, 200 mcg of chromium picolinate, GammaFrac (Ferulic Acid
Complex), DMG (125 mg of n,n dimethyl glycine), and sometimes a
multivitamin (I favor diversity, figuring that multivitamins from >2
manufacturers will have more chance of hitting the bases).

- Oh, and I wake up in the morning to a caffeine tablet (no apologies
here, as it's faster than coffee, which I don't care for....and I
don't get caffeine jitters--it just sharpens my attention and makes me
feel alert and energetic). I also take an asprin every day, for blood
clot lessening, cancer reduction, etc. Sometimes I take ephedrine, in
nasal decongestant form, which increases thermogenic fat-burning.

- On the downside, I'm not a vegetarian (or a "Vegan"), though I often
go a day without eating any meat. I rarely buy milk...when I do, it
sits undrunk in my refrigerator until I dump it out. I eat cheese on
pizza, in salads, etc. (I have no lactose intolerance, being a
Viking-American.) 

- I bought a juicer and try to use it. I am persuaded that eating a
lot of fruit is a good thing.

(My grocery habits are shifting more toward grains and vegetables with the
opening of a "Trader Joe's" nearby...they have no _fresh_ vegetable,
but all kinds of dried fruits, grains, nuts, and prepared dishes like
tabbouli, tofu dishes, etc. The also have the best prices on cheeses,
so I'm always tempted.)

- Some days I drink red wine, some days I don't. I've been cutting way
back on beer, rarely buying it (when the price of Heinekens hit $5 a
6-a few years back, I stopped buying it...now it's $7, what with all
the "sin taxes" on it....California wine remains a good deal).

(I almost never see wine and alcohol mentioned here. Do people believe
or disbelieve the various studies about the healthful effects of one
or two drinks a day? Not just the antioxidant properties of wine
(grape skins have antifungals, and so on), but also the effects of
moderate amounts of straight alcohol.)

My overall health is good, so far as I know (I haven't been to a
doctor since 1970...perhaps this is foolish...if so, don't bother to
lecture me about it!). I'm 41, 6'2", 210 pounds (I need to lose 10
pounds, in my opinion, mostly in my gut). My workout is mostly
upper-body strength related, on barbells and Nautilus-type machines.
Very little aerobics, though I recently bought a stair climber machine
for home use on days I don't go the gym...unfortunately, it's all too
easy to skip this! (I suppose I do the strength routines for vanity
reasons...it feels good to keep upping the weights on the machines and
seeing my body change shape. I suspect the aerobic exercise are more
healthy...they're just so goddamn _boring_. Besides, I've read that
having more muscle mass, in the legs, for example, is just as healthy,
because the body must then work these muscles throughout the day. Your
mileage may vary, literally.)

THE MAY EQUATION

As I've mentioned before, I'm slowly working on a spreadsheet model
for my "May Equation," a parallel to the "Drake Equation." I try to
take the latest information, use some personal judgment to correct for
hyperbole, and incorporate it into my "equation."

MAY EQUATION (sketchy form):

Extra Years of Quality Life = (Quality of Life Term x Extra Years) +
                              (Probability of Reanimation x Extra
                              Years after Reanimation x Quality of
                              Life after Reanimation)

Each of these main terms expands into many subcomponents...some are
highly uncertain. Some depend on age (my extra years, at 41, may be
less those of someone now 25...on the other hand, lifespans have *not*
been increasing much, once infant mortality and accidents are taken
out of the equation).

The spreadsheet form (I started in Lotus 1-2-3/Mac, but am moving it
over to SpreadBase, a category-based program for the Mac) allows me to
break the model into as many components as I think are needed, and
then to adjust each one based on what I read and learn.

(I once suggested on this list that a "Composite Model" be developed
by interested Extropians, incorporating whatever we can learn about
life extension, but got no response. So I'm doing it alone.)

The "effort" needed to gain these additional years is an important
issue...it may set a "crossover" point. For example, suppose one
spends 2 hours in a gym, 4 times a week (an hour of workout, an hour
to get to/from/shower/etc.). Over the next 30 years, this represents
12,000 hours of "quality time" lost. There are about 15 hours a day of
quality time (and even less of "personal time," after working hours
are taken out...and exercise time must come out of personal time), so
this 12,000 hours of exercise time is 800 days = 2 years.

So, if I spend all this time exercising and only buy a year of extra
life, it's a lose. (There are other benefits to exercise, such as
better health, better muscle tone, and so on. These would need to be
estimated...I haven't figured out the best way to do this.)

(It gets much worse if 2-4 hours a day are spent on a special diet, as
someone who tried the "Walford Diet," or somesuch, mentioned. I'm not
knocking vegan, of course, just pointing this out.)

Will exercise or diet "significantly" increase lifespan? Who knows. I
hope to include terms for this in my model, though.

I must say I'm deeply skeptical about things like the "120 year diet."
There may be a very few (4-5 sigmas out) people who live to be
110-120, but not much evidence of diet as the cause (ever notice how
many of these folks lived on chitlins and grits?). The
"semi-starvation" diet may or may not work, and I expect to follow
this. (I expect semi-starvation will cause a big downgrade of my
"quality of life" correction factor, though.)  

I suspect that for people who already don't smoke, don't drink too
much, get at least some exercise, and aren't obese, the "extra years"
obtainable with the best diets and exercise (not clear what that is,
of course) for someone now 30 years old is, very roughly, about 1-3
years. That is , I don't expect any miracle diet will make a 30-year
old, with a life expectancy of 80 to instead have an expectancy of
much beyond 83 or so. (Sure, many advances in medicine can occur.
These are not caused by the diet, per se, and may or may not occur, so
I can't include them in the diet+exercise equation!).

I would be very happy if evidence appears that diet + exercise can
increase the life expectancy to, say, 90.

Well, this is all I have to say for now....it should be enough!

-Tim May


